User Stories
User Story 1: Power Management
As a user, I want the Pocket Professor to turn on and off reliably so that I can control when it is active and available for use.
Tasks: 	
Research Raspberry Pi power management options (1-2 days)
Install power management components (1-2 days)
Test power on/off cycling (1 day)
Implement safe shutdown mechanism (1 day)

Resources Needed: 
Raspberry Pi power adapter or battery pack
Wiring and connectors
1-2 team members (Hardware setup)

Dependencies: None (foundational task)
	





User Story 2: Visual Perception Setup
As a user, I want the Pocket Professor to see my workspace so that it can understand what I am showing it and provide helpful guidance.

Tasks: 	
Research Raspberry Pi camera compatibility and options (1day)
Install camera hardware on Pi (1 Day)
Configure camera drivers and test image capture (2 days)
Optimize camera for lighting and clarity (1 day)
Set up live video streaming capability (2 days)

Resources needed:
Raspberry Pi Camera Module (or USB camera)
Camera cables and mounting hardware (Mount can be printed)
1-2 team members (Hardware + Software setup)

Dependencies: User story 1 (Power must be working!)

User Story 3: Physical Housing and Structure
As a user, I want the Pocket Professor to have a compact and stable physical design so that it fits on my workspace and resembles a professor.

Tasks: 	
Design 3D model of professor figure in CAD (3-4 days)
Create head (with camera mount cavity)
Design body (with Raspberry Pi mounting points and ventilation holes)
Design arms/legs (optional aesthetic elements like a coffee mug)
Add cable routing channels internally
Export design files for 3D printing (STL/OBJ format) (1 day)
Test print prototype parts (small scale or single component) (1-2 days)
Check fit, tolerance, and print quality
Identify and fix design issues
Print full-scale body components (4-6 days)
Head piece with camera opening
Main body shell (may print in multiple sections if large)
Base/legs for stability
Mounting brackets/supports for internal hardware
Post-processing printed parts (2 days)
Remove support material
Sand/finish surfaces for smooth appearance
Paint or apply finishing (optional but recommended)
Assemble 3D printed components (1-2 days)
Bond parts together with adhesive or fasteners
Install internal mounting brackets
Ensure structural integrity
Integrate electronics into housing (2 days)
Mount Raspberry Pi securely
Install camera in head cavity
Route cables through internal channels
Secure microphone and speaker mounts
Test stability, balance, and stand capability (1 day)
Verify unit stands upright without support
Check weight distribution
Ensure no tipping or wobbling




Resources needed:
CAD software (FreeCAD, Fusion 360, TinkerCAD, MeshMixer)
3D printer access (School)
PLA or PETG filament (500g-1kg estimated)
Post-processing materials: sandpaper, paint/primer, super glue or epoxy
Small fasteners (screws, nuts) for assembly
Cable ties/clips for internal routing
1-2 team members (CAD designer + print management + assembly)


Dependencies: 
User story 2 (Camera specifications needed for head cavity design)
3D printer availability
User Story 1 (Power system finalized for internal routing)



User Story 4: Voice Input Capability
As a user, I want to ask the Pocket Professor questions by speaking so that I can interact with it hands-free while working.

Tasks: 	
Research Raspberry Pi microphone compatibility and options (1 day)
Select and order USB (or different) microphone (1 day)
Install microphone hardware (2 days)
Configure audio input drivers (1 day)
Set up speech-to-text conversion library (2 days)
Test voice recognition accuracy (1-2 days)

Resources needed:
USB microphone or other microphone module
Audio cables
Speech-to-text library
1-2 team members (Hardware + Software)

Dependencies: User story 1 (Power must be working!)

User Story 5: Voice Output Response
As a user, I want the Pocket Professor to speak its responses so that I can receive guidance without needing to look at a screen.

Tasks: 	
Research Raspberry Pi speaker/audio output options (1 day)
Select and order speaker or USB audio module (1 day)
Install audio output hardware (1 day)
Configure audio output drivers (1 day)
Set up text-to-speech system (2 days)
Test voice quality and volume levels (1 day)
Optimize audio playback for clarity (1 day)



Resources needed:
Small speaker module or USB audio adapter
Audio cables
Text-to-speech library (open-source)
1-2 team members (Hardware + software)

Dependencies: User story 1 (Power must be working!)
